{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 RCA (rebar-debug) — PROVEN, cited; disposition ESCALATE (no code changed)\n\n**Proven root cause.** `_reads._tracker()` (`src/rebar/_reads.py:42`) uses `if not\nos.path.isdir(tracker)` as its SOLE initialization predicate. A directory that exists but is\nnot a usable store satisfies `isdir`, so the guard never fires and the read proceeds; one layer\ndown the reducer's `except OSError: return results` swallows the failure and yields `[]`. The\ncaller cannot tell \"empty store\" from \"broken store\".\n\n**Runtime confirmation (this worktree, off origin/main `dddaa60`).** Drove the real\n`rebar.list_tickets(repo_root=…)` entry point:\n- Case 1 (production shape) — tracker dir with a `reviewbot-ensure-tickets` marker file and NO\n  `.git`: returned `[]` (should raise `store_uninitialized`).\n- Case 3 (mid-clone) — `.git` present but HEAD unresolvable (`git rev-parse --verify HEAD` rc\n  128): returned `[]` (should raise `store_uninitialized`).\n- Control — an `init_repo` store (has `.git` + resolvable HEAD, zero tickets) reads `[]`\n  correctly, and must keep doing so (`tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py`).\n\n**Why it is a defect (cited authority).**\n- The write side already guards usability: `event_prepare._ensure_initialized`\n  (`src/rebar/_store/event_prepare.py:101`) raises when `not isdir(tracker) or not\n  exists(tracker/.git)`. Reads check only `isdir`, so read/write disagree about the same store.\n- `docs/mcp-reference.md` forbids fallback-masking (faults must surface as errors, not resolve\n  to a silent fallback); a broken store reading `[]` is exactly that.\n- The existing contract test pins the distinction (\"directory does not exist\" = error vs\n  \"exists and holds no tickets\" = `[]`).\n- Standing intent is documented by prior closed tickets: `antelopine-limivorous-chinchilla`\n  (introduced the guard + the `store_uninitialized` code), `fathomable-yester-thrip` (recorded\n  this as a residual), and `unfit-beneficial-whimbrel` AC3 (\"while the clone is in flight,\n  tracker reads report `store_uninitialized` rather than `[]`\" — explicitly left unticked,\n  pointing here).\n\n**Defect origin / `caused_by`.** Attributed to `44fd-230c-5893-4246`\n(`antelopine-limivorous-chinchilla`), introducing commit `84fc5f9b68df8` (the line\n`if not os.path.isdir(tracker):`). That change added the read guard but keyed it on directory\npresence only, not store usability — an incomplete implementation of the guard's own intent\n(and of parity with the write side, which already checked `.git`). `caused_by` link recorded.\nClass if closed: `plan_defect` / incomplete-implementation.\n\n**Caller-dependency gate.** Only consumer of the `store_uninitialized` code is\n`rebar.audit.server._audited_tickets` (`src/rebar/audit/server.py:99-102`), which catches that\ncode and degrades to an empty index while propagating every OTHER error. Extending the guard to\nemit `store_uninitialized` for broken/mid-clone stores keeps that caller consistent (it degrades\nrather than 500s). No high-confidence behavioral-dependency caller relies on the current `[]`.\n\n**Why ESCALATE rather than auto-land.** This is a genuine defect with documented standing\nintent, but the correct fix is NOT behavior-preserving and carries a design choice, so under the\norchestrator's escalation rules it needs human authorization:\n1. It changes observable read semantics **repo-wide** — all 7 library/MCP read entry points that\n   funnel through `_tracker()` now raise for a class of inputs that previously returned `[]`.\n   The ticket itself flags this blast radius as the reason it was scoped out of change 2244.\n2. The \"usable store\" predicate is a design tradeoff: `.env-id` is git-ignored LOCAL state\n   (absent immediately after a valid clone — would misclassify a fresh valid clone as\n   uninitialized; cf. `ab61-f3e8-6ee6-4905`), so the read-side discriminator should be a\n   **resolvable git HEAD** (matches AC3 and the mid-clone case), which adds a HEAD-resolution on\n   the read hot path and raises the question of unifying with the write-side check.\n\n**Recommended fix (for the authorizing human).** In `_reads._tracker()`, after the `isdir`\ncheck, also treat the store as uninitialized (same `store_uninitialized` code + wording) when it\nis not a usable store — i.e. `.git` absent OR HEAD not resolvable (`git rev-parse --verify -q\nHEAD`). Keep the existing empty-initialized-store contract test passing unmodified, add regression\ncoverage for (a) present dir without `.git`, (b) `.git` present but HEAD unresolvable (mid-clone),\nand (c) a guard that fails if the predicate reverts to bare `os.path.isdir`. Consider factoring the\nusability predicate so read and write sides share it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787938141287821001,"uuid":"ac453ef7-9a0c-4ecc-af20-2566c03980eb"}